# What the license covers

The MIT terms in [LICENSE](LICENSE) cover the **software** in this repository:

- `glee_agent/` — the agent
- `tests/` — behavioral and analysis tests, including reference test configuration
- `tools/` — setup check, transcript reader, post-mortem analysis
- `paper/analysis/` — the analysis scripts behind the paper's numbers
- `paper/build.py`, `paper/gen_fig.py` and `paper/submitted/build.py`

They do **not** cover the paper. "Below the Noise Floor: A Deterministic GLEE
Agent and the Measurement Problem It Exposed" — its camera-ready and submitted
PDFs, its LaTeX sources (`paper/glee_competition_paper.tex`,
`paper/fig_noise.tex` and the `.tex` files in `paper/submitted/`),
the manuscript text inside `paper/make_camera_ready.py`, and the change register
(`paper/change-register.html`) — remains Copyright (c) 2026 Yosef
Zoabi, all rights reserved. It is included so that readers of the paper can
audit the work behind it. Quote it as you would any paper, under normal
academic citation.

`paper/neurips_2026.sty` (and its copy in `paper/submitted/`) is the official
NeurIPS 2026 style file, redistributed
unmodified under its own terms, and is covered by neither of the above.

# Data

Only aggregate diagnostics, rounded plotted coordinates (with the Figure 1
values decoded from them) and per-slot game counters are included; no raw game transcripts are included. The turn logs also carry
the transcripts of the agents I was matched against, so they are not published
here. The competition organizers have said they will release the full game
record for every agent. That would not by itself recover local joins, polling
snapshots, deployment flags, or the original replay pipeline.
`paper/analysis/README.md` documents the shape of the input each script
expects, so the method can be audited ahead of that release.
